---
name: dev-task
version: 3.17.0
description: lead dev assignment for a planned dev bead in atm-bd-orchestration. The bead is the assignment; task_id is the bead id.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - sprint
  - stack
  - pr_target
  - worktree_path
  - branch
  - primary_checkout
  - phase_root
  - test_command
  - lint_command
  - policy_path
metadata:
  type: dev-task
  tags:
    - stage:dev
    - orchestration:beads
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: sprint
      variable: sprint
    state: dev-start
    stage: dev
    transition: start
---
<atm-task id="{{ task_id }}" sprint="{{ sprint }}">
  <description><![CDATA[Implement dev bead `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (sprint {{ sprint | string | cdata_escape }}). `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` is the assignment: its description, design, acceptance criteria and metadata are authoritative.]]></description>

  <worktree><![CDATA[{{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}]]></worktree>
  <branch><![CDATA[{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}]]></branch>
  <stack><![CDATA[{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}]]></stack>
  <pr-target><![CDATA[{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}]]></pr-target>
  <stack-discipline><![CDATA[`{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` is cut from `origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}`, top of stack `{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}` at dispatch; its PR lands on the stack's current top. Work only on this branch. The task assigner is the stack writer: never run `gh stack link`, `sync`, `rebase` or `merge`. Once linked the layer is frozen; later changes are fix beads.]]></stack-discipline>

  <workflow>
    <step id="a"><![CDATA[Before claiming, run `git fetch origin && git rebase origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` in the worktree (a conflict is part of the fix), then `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/assignment-gates.py dev --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }} --bead {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --pr-target {{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} --worktree {{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}` from the worktree (scripts come from the primary checkout, never the branch). Checks, commands and refusal codes: (1) plan valid: `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-beads/scripts/validate-plan --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }}` exits 0, else `PLAN_INVALID`; (2) ready: `bd ready -n 0 --json` lists the bead, else `NOT_READY`; (3) claimable without `--force`: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --json` shows status `open` and assignee empty or `$ATM_IDENTITY`, else `UNCLAIMABLE`; (4) target: `metadata.pr_target` is `{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` or `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/<metadata.pr_target> origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` exits 0 (`pr_target` is a lower bound), else `PR_TARGET_MISMATCH`; (5) base: `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} HEAD` exits 0, else `WRONG_BASE`; `GATE_CANNOT_RUN` when a command fails. On refusal before claiming, follow step a1 for `NOT_READY`; otherwise close the task `refused` with the step g1 template, leaving the bead unchanged and including the refusal code, command and failure evidence in `reason_md`. On success claim with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --claim --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"`, then start `atm task start {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} "<one line: what you will do first>"`.]]></step>
    <step id="a1"><![CDATA[When it prints `NOT_READY` the bead is not ready: do not claim it or start the task. Find the root cause: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (status, assignee), `bd blocked --json | jq --arg b {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} '.[] | select(.id == $b)'` (open blockers), and `bd show <blocker>` for each (status, assignee, what it waits on). Then refuse, never wait: close the task with `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} refused --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/task-refused.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/refused-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`, `bead_state` `open`, `reason_md` naming the bead, why it is not ready, which bead or agent must move, and, for a blocker not yet its dependency, the edge to add, `bd dep add {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --blocked-by <blocker>`, so `bd ready` lists the bead only once the blocker closes. The task assigner re-assigns it then.]]></step>
    <step id="c"><![CDATA[Before coding, read `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}`: it is authoritative for scope, deliverables, deletion targets, validation, and acceptance criteria, and `metadata.owned_paths` is your file fence. A field the bead lacks (a poured dev bead's deliverables, `owned_paths`, `requirements`, `adrs`) comes from its sprint container: `bd show <metadata.sprint_bead>`. If this assignment and the bead differ, the bead wins; report the mismatch. Then, before coding, read every id in `metadata.requirements` and `metadata.adrs` in the governing documents; your change must satisfy each, and QA checks it against them. `NONE` means the plan declares none; if the work touches a requirement or ADR anyway, report it to the task assigner before coding past it.]]></step>
    <step id="c0"><![CDATA[Read the repository QA policy `{{ policy_path | string | cdata_escape }}` before editing, and every guideline it names for the languages this task touches; the reviewers score against them.]]></step>
    <step id="d"><![CDATA[Work only from the bead's deliverables and acceptance criteria. Read the whole plan, enumerate every task required in the primary and any child beads, then create an itemized private checklist outside the tracked tree with every task identified, and work through the checklist one item at a time. Commit on `{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` as items land; push early (first `git push -u origin {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}`) so work is never only local. A push is not a close and needs no report. No user is watching or will approve a step: never state what you will do next, then stop for someone to say go. While the task is active never end a turn on a promise of future work: a turn that ends is idle, so execute each step and continue until the close (step g) or a refusal.]]></step>
    <step id="d1"><![CDATA[If the work needs a change other live branches need at the same time (a shared type or trait signature, or a critical bug every branch carries), stop that edit, tell the task assigner the exact change and the branches it breaks, follow the assigner's base (`.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/SKILL.md`, Parallel Quick Fix), and report a quick-fix PR you open by plain `atm send`.]]></step>
    <step id="e"><![CDATA[Export `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4` for every cargo and just invocation in this task: five devs share this host and 20-30 parallel rustc processes starve the beads database (Rand 2026-10-20). Finish every item, then go through the checklist again one item at a time, confirming each is fully met; never weaken a test, skip a criterion, or leave a placeholder. Run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}`, `{{ lint_command | string | cdata_escape }}` and the bead's validation commands until they pass with zero failures; a lint failure inherited from a lower layer is yours to fix on this layer.]]></step>
    <step id="f"><![CDATA[Rebase onto the stack's current top (it may have moved since dispatch): `git fetch origin`; the top is what `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/assignment-gates.py stack-top --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }} --pr-target {{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} --worktree {{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}` prints on exit 0 (from the phase stack bead); any other exit prints `GATE_CANNOT_RUN`: never guess, refuse with it by step g1. Then `git rebase origin/<top>`, then re-validate only what the rebase can break: `cargo fmt --all --check` and `cargo clippy -p <each crate you changed> --all-targets -- -D warnings`, plus that crate's tests only if the rebase reported conflicts in it; never re-run the full `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}` or `{{ lint_command | string | cdata_escape }}` after a rebase (the stack top moves faster than a cold full-suite run; CI on the stack top is the full-suite gate, Rand 2026-10-20). Then `git push --force-with-lease origin {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}`. If the top moves again before the push, push anyway and name the base in the report; the assigner restacks. Write the PR description vars to `<scratch>/{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-pr-body.json`: `task_id` = `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}`; `summary_md`, a complete description of the work done; `deliverables`, one entry per numbered bead deliverable in bead order, each saying what was delivered and where; `generated_by` = `<ATM_IDENTITY>@<ATM_TEAM> <harness>:<model>`, the harness and model from your pane entry in `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.atm.toml` (`--model gpt-6.1-luna` is `codex:luna`, `model = "sonnet"` is `claude:sonnet`; with no pane entry, your own harness and model). Render it: `sc-compose render --file {{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/pr-body.md.j2 --var-file <scratch>/{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-pr-body.json --strict --output <scratch>/{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-pr-body.md` (a failure: fix the vars and re-render). Then open the PR against that top: `gh pr create --base <top> --head {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }} --title "<one-line title>" --body-file <scratch>/{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-pr-body.md` (never `--draft`; an open PR on the branch gets `gh pr edit <number> --body-file <scratch>/{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-pr-body.md` instead), run `/sc-gh-stack-view` (read-only) and keep its output verbatim. Record the full head SHA, the top as `rebased_onto`, and the PR number and url.]]></step>
    <step id="g"><![CDATA[Complete through the hook only: run `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/atm-task-close.py --role dev --vars <lead-supplied-vars-file> --result <your-result-file>`. Pass the lead's immutable shared dispatch vars file unchanged: never add completion fields to it. Write `<your-result-file>` as JSON with only your own fields: `deliverables_complete`, `deliverables_md`, `deliverables_total`, `evidence`, `outcome`, `stack_view` (verbatim `/sc-gh-stack-view` output), `validation_md`. The hook derives `commit`, `pr_number`, `pr_url`, `rebased_onto` and `generated_at` from git and the open PR at HEAD; never type them. If it lacks required values, ask the lead to supply the correct file. On `err`, fix the named cause and run the hook again. On `ok`, the hook has closed the bead, assigned sanity as the lead, and sent the completion report; it has also closed your ATM task as completed, so you are free to start your next queued task. Sanity runs on its own task. A `sanity-fail` or `rebase-required` message means the lead re-assigns this task: start it, address the cause, rebase onto the current stack top, re-run validation, push, and run the same hook again with the lead-supplied vars file. Do not close the completed bead or task by hand.]]></step>
    <step id="g1"><![CDATA[If the bead cannot be completed at all, include the reason, command and failure evidence in the refusal's `reason_md`. Announce a serious infrastructure failure once per cause to the oversight recipients (`.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/SKILL.md` Lead Role). Declare failure with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status blocked --assignee "" --append-notes "DEV_CANNOT_COMPLETE; <reason>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` (never `bd close`: a closed dev bead releases its sanity check) and `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} refused --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/task-refused.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/refused-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. An unfinished deliverable is a failure, not a completion. A blocker (work another bead or agent owns is not done: a prerequisite open or reopened, another agent's fix not landed) is not a failure: stop, return the bead with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status open --assignee "" --append-notes "BLOCKED: <blocker>: <why>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` and close the task `refused` with the same command (`bead_state` `open`), `reason_md` naming the blocker and the edge `bd dep add {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --blocked-by <blocker>`; never stay active waiting. A shared change you can make yourself is not a blocker: it is a Parallel Quick Fix (step d1) and the task continues. Only a genuine cannot-complete uses `--status blocked`.]]></step>
    <step id="h"><![CDATA[After the close, read ATM again: the next queued task starts only now.]]></step>
  </workflow>
</atm-task>
